Manage Long-lived and Short-lived Applications Using Kubernetes Workload API
Objective
Learn how Kubernetes manages applications using workload resources.
Applications in Kubernetes can be:
Type	Meaning
Short-lived applications	Run once and stop
Long-lived applications	Run continuously
These are managed using the Kubernetes Workloads API.
________________________________________
1. Kubernetes Workload Resources
The Workloads API contains resources used to run and manage applications in the cluster.
Most commonly used resources:
Resource	Purpose
Job	Run a task once
CronJob	Run task on schedule
Deployment	Run long-lived applications
DaemonSet	Run pod on every node
StatefulSet	Run stateful applications
These resources manage pods automatically.
________________________________________
2. Jobs (Short-Lived Workloads)
A Job runs a one-time task in Kubernetes.
Example uses:
•	Database migration
•	Backup
•	Batch processing
•	One-time script execution
A Job creates a pod that runs the task.
If the pod fails → Kubernetes retries automatically.
When the job completes successfully → it stops.
________________________________________
Job vs Pod
Feature	Pod (oc run)	Job
Restart on failure	❌ No	✅ Yes
Controller manages it	❌ No	✅ Yes
Retry capability	❌ No	✅ Yes
________________________________________
Create a Job
Command:
oc create job date-job \
--image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi \
-- /bin/bash -c "date"
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc create job	create job resource
date-job	job name
--image	container image
--	separates command
date	command executed in container
Result:
Kubernetes creates a pod that runs:
date
Then the pod exits after printing the date.
________________________________________
3. CronJobs (Scheduled Jobs)
A CronJob runs a job periodically based on time.
Works similar to Linux crontab.
Example uses:
•	Nightly backups
•	Log cleanup
•	Report generation
•	Scheduled maintenance
________________________________________
Cron Format
* * * * *
| | | | |
| | | | +---- day of week
| | | +------ month
| | +-------- day of month
| +---------- hour
+------------ minute
Example:
*/1 * * * *
Meaning:
Run every minute.
________________________________________
Create CronJob
Command:
oc create cronjob date-cronjob \
--image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi \
--schedule "*/1 * * * *" \
-- date
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc create cronjob	create scheduled job
date-cronjob	name
--image	container image
--schedule	cron timing
date	command executed
Result:
Every 1 minute, Kubernetes:
1.	Creates a Job
2.	Job creates a Pod
3.	Pod runs date
________________________________________
4. Deployments (Long-Lived Applications)
A Deployment manages long-running applications.
Example:
•	Web servers
•	APIs
•	Microservices
•	Applications
Deployment ensures:
•	required number of pods always run
•	pods restart if they crash
•	rolling updates
________________________________________
Deployment Architecture
Deployment
   ↓
ReplicaSet
   ↓
Pods
Explanation:
Component	Purpose
Deployment	manages application
ReplicaSet	maintains number of pods
Pod	runs containers
________________________________________
Create Deployment
Command:
oc create deployment my-deployment \
--image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi \
--replicas 3
Explanation
Part	Meaning
oc create deployment	create deployment
my-deployment	name
--image	container image
--replicas 3	run 3 pods
Result:
Kubernetes creates:
1 Deployment
1 ReplicaSet
3 Pods
________________________________________
Why Deployment is Important
If a pod crashes:
Kubernetes automatically creates another pod.
Example:
Replicas = 3
Pod crashes → new pod created
So deployment ensures high availability.
________________________________________
5. Labels and Selectors
Labels are key-value metadata attached to resources.
Example:
environment=testing
team=frontend
layer=backend
Labels help:
•	organize resources
•	filter resources
•	connect services and pods
________________________________________
Example Label
layer=frontend
Applied to UI components.
________________________________________
Delete Pods with Label
Command:
oc delete pod -l environment=testing
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
-l	label selector
environment=testing	filter condition
Deletes only pods with that label.
________________________________________
Selectors
Selectors are queries used to match labels.
Example YAML:
selector:
  matchLabels:
    app: httpd
Meaning:
Select pods where:
app=httpd
________________________________________
6. DaemonSets
A DaemonSet runs a pod on every node in the cluster.
Example:
Cluster with 3 nodes:
Node1 → Pod
Node2 → Pod
Node3 → Pod
If a new node is added:
Node4 → Pod automatically created
________________________________________
Uses of DaemonSets
Common use cases:
Use Case	Example
Logging agents	Fluentd
Monitoring agents	Prometheus node exporter
Storage services	Ceph
Networking services	CNI plugins
________________________________________
Create DaemonSet
Command:
oc create daemonset my-daemonset \
--image registry.access.redhat.com/ubi9/ubi
Explanation:
Part	Meaning
oc create daemonset	create daemonset
my-daemonset	name
--image	container image
Result:
A pod runs on every node.
________________________________________
7. StatefulSets
A StatefulSet manages stateful applications.
Example:
•	databases
•	distributed systems
•	clustered apps
Unlike deployments:
Pods are not identical.
Each pod has:
•	unique hostname
•	unique storage
•	stable identity
________________________________________
Example Stateful Pods
mysql-0
mysql-1
mysql-2
Each pod:
•	keeps its own data
•	has persistent storage
________________________________________
Deployment vs StatefulSet
Feature	Deployment	StatefulSet
Pod identity	identical	unique
Storage	shared	persistent per pod
Use case	stateless apps	databases
________________________________________
Long-Lived vs Short-Lived Workloads
Workload Type	Resource
One-time task	Job
Scheduled task	CronJob
Continuous service	Deployment
Node-level service	DaemonSet
Stateful application	StatefulSet
